{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfrZE4mrnLPDrVhrM299F", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Salt on the Glass\n\nThe light at Carrow Point had been burning for a hundred and nine years, and for the last thirty of them Edwin Hale had kept it. He kept the lamp trimmed and the lens polished, wound the clockwork that turned the great prisms every night, and wrote the weather in the log three times a day in a hand that had grown smaller as his eyes weakened. Nobody had asked him to make the log legible to anyone but himself. Still, he wrote it carefully, the way he did most things, as though someone might one day need to read exactly what the sea had done on a particular Tuesday in March.\n\nThe station had been automated in the spring of the year his wife died. The service had sent a letter, polite and final, saying the keeper's position was no longer required, that a sensor array and a radio beacon would do the work more reliably, and that a pension arrangement had been made. Edwin had read the letter at the kitchen table in town, folded it into thirds, and put it in the drawer with the rubber bands and the warranty for a toaster they no longer owned. Then he had driven back out to the point, because the lamp was still there and he did not know how to be anywhere that the lamp was not.\n\nThe service had changed its mind after a fishing boat went onto the Teeth in a fog that the beacon, for reasons nobody fully understood, had failed to report. Two men were lost. The inquiry found that the array had been reporting normally, which everyone agreed was the problem: it had reported a healthy light when the light had,"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 196, "cache_read_input_tokens": 36673, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 196}, "